{
  "$schema": "../contract.schema.json",
  "id": "al-header",
  "name": "Header",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "status": "derived",
  "description": "Component: al-header\n\nSouthleft's primary navigation bar — sticky, translucent, blurred, with pill\nnav items, circular icon actions and a numbered mobile panel.\n\nIT *IS* `al-header` — the brand implementation of that tag, not a wrapper\naround Altitude's. It owns the `<header>` landmark, the sticky behaviour and\nthe stacking context itself, and adds what is Southleft's alone: the pill\ntreatment, the icon-button shape, and the mobile panel.\n\nIt used to compose `<al-header>` and feed it chrome through\n`--al-header-background` / `-backdrop-filter` / `-border-block-end`. Taking\nthe tag ended that (one namespace, spec\n2026-08-23-one-al-namespace-across-brand-and-base): an `<al-header>` rendered\ninside `<al-header>` recurses forever. Those custom properties are still the\npublic API — header.scss now READS them rather than passing them on — so a\nconsumer overriding one is unaffected.\n\nAn app gets this implementation, or Altitude's bare landmark, by importing\none module or the other. Never both: `customElements.define` is first-come\nand final.\n\n```html\n<al-header>\n  <al-logo slot=\"brand\" variant=\"southleft\" href=\"/\"></al-logo>\n  <a slot=\"nav\" href=\"/work\">Work</a>\n  <a slot=\"nav\" href=\"/insights\" aria-current=\"page\">Insights</a>\n  <button slot=\"actions\" aria-label=\"Ink / paper\">…</button>\n  <al-button slot=\"actions\" href=\"/contact\">Book a call</al-button>\n  <a slot=\"mobile\" href=\"/work\">Work</a>\n</al-header>\n```\n\nIt earns its own tag by owning BEHAVIOUR, not arrangement: the mobile panel's\nopen/closed state, the button that drives it, and the `aria-expanded` /\n`aria-controls` relationship between them. Everything positional is still\n`<al-layout>`.",
  "semantics": {
    "element": "header",
    "role": null
  },
  "props": [
    {
      "name": "menu-open",
      "description": "Whether the mobile panel is open. Reflected so the page can style against\n`al-header[menu-open]` and so the state is inspectable in devtools rather\nthan trapped in the shadow root.",
      "type": "boolean",
      "rawType": "boolean",
      "bindings": {
        "code": {
          "attribute": "menu-open",
          "member": "menuOpen"
        },
        "figma": null
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "sticky",
      "description": "Stick the bar to the top of the scroll container. On by default — the site's header is sticky.",
      "type": "boolean",
      "rawType": "boolean",
      "default": "true",
      "bindings": {
        "code": {
          "attribute": "sticky"
        },
        "figma": null
      }
    }
  ],
  "events": [
    {
      "name": "sl-header-menu-toggle",
      "description": "When the mobile panel opens or closes. `detail.open` carries the new state."
    }
  ],
  "slots": [
    {
      "name": "actions",
      "description": "The right-hand cluster. Native `<button>`s get the circular icon treatment; anything else (an `<al-button>` CTA) is left alone."
    },
    {
      "name": "brand",
      "description": "The wordmark. Usually `<al-logo variant=\"southleft\">`."
    },
    {
      "name": "mobile",
      "description": "The panel's links. Supplied separately from `nav` because a slot can project its nodes only once — see the note in the SCSS."
    },
    {
      "name": "nav",
      "description": "The primary links, as flat `<a>` elements. The component gives them the pill treatment and marks `aria-current=\"page\"` as the filled state. Hidden below the `64rem` breakpoint, where the mobile panel takes over."
    }
  ],
  "states": [
    "hover",
    "focus",
    "active",
    "disabled"
  ],
